[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Working with Different Database Systems (PostgreSQL, SQLite) in PHP"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP offers excellent support for interacting with various database systems. This tutorial will guide you through connecting to and performing basic operations on PostgreSQL and SQLite databases using PHP's built-in functions and PDO (PHP Data Objects)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PHP installation (version 7.0 or higher recommended)"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PostgreSQL and SQLite database servers installed and running"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"PDO extensions for PostgreSQL (pdo_pgsql) and SQLite (pdo_sqlite) enabled in your php.ini file.  Uncomment the lines: 'extension=pdo_pgsql' and 'extension=pdo_sqlite'"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting to PostgreSQL"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"PostgreSQL is a powerful, open-source relational database system. To connect to a PostgreSQL database from PHP using PDO, you'll need the connection string, username, and password."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$host = 'localhost';
$port = '5432';
$dbname = 'mydatabase';
$user = 'myuser';
$password = 'mypassword';

try {
    $pdo = new PDO("pgsql:host=$host;port=$port;dbname=$dbname", $user, $password);
    $pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);
    echo "Connected to PostgreSQL successfully!";
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    echo "Connection failed: " . $e->getMessage();
}

?>"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:
- The connection string specifies the database type (pgsql), host, port, and database name.
- The PDO constructor takes the connection string, username, and password as arguments.
- 'PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE' is set to 'PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION' to enable exception handling for database errors."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Connecting to SQLite"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"SQLite is a lightweight, file-based database system. Connecting to an SQLite database is simpler than PostgreSQL because it doesn't require a separate server process."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"<?php
$dbfile = 'mydatabase.db';

try {
    $pdo = new PDO("sqlite:" . $dbfile);
    $pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);
    echo "Connected to SQLite successfully!";
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    echo "Connection failed: " . $e->getMessage();
}

?>"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:
- The connection string specifies the database type (sqlite) and the path to the database file.
- If the database file doesn't exist, SQLite will create it automatically."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Performing Basic Database Operations (CRUD)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once connected, you can perform CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations using PDO's prepared statements. This example demonstrates these operations for both PostgreSQL and SQLite (the code is largely the same, only the connection differs)."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"<?php
// Database connection (replace with your PostgreSQL or SQLite connection code)
$dbfile = 'mydatabase.db'; //For SQLite
$pdo = new PDO("sqlite:" . $dbfile); //For SQLite
//$host = 'localhost'; //For PostgreSQL
//$port = '5432'; //For PostgreSQL
//$dbname = 'mydatabase'; //For PostgreSQL
//$user = 'myuser'; //For PostgreSQL
//$password = 'mypassword'; //For PostgreSQL
//$pdo = new PDO("pgsql:host=$host;port=$port;dbname=$dbname", $user, $password); //For PostgreSQL
$pdo->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);

// Create (Insert)
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES (:name, :email)");
$stmt->execute(['name' => 'John Doe', 'email' => 'john.doe@example.com']);
$lastInsertId = $pdo->lastInsertId();
echo "Inserted new record with ID: " . $lastInsertId . "\n";

// Read (Select)
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = :id");
$stmt->execute(['id' => $lastInsertId]);
$user = $stmt->fetch(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);
print_r($user);

// Update
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("UPDATE users SET email = :email WHERE id = :id");
$stmt->execute(['email' => 'john.updated@example.com', 'id' => $lastInsertId]);
echo "Updated record with ID: " . $lastInsertId . "\n";

// Delete
$stmt = $pdo->prepare("DELETE FROM users WHERE id = :id");
$stmt->execute(['id' => $lastInsertId]);
echo "Deleted record with ID: " . $lastInsertId . "\n";

?>"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Explanation:
- Prepared statements prevent SQL injection attacks by separating the SQL code from the data.
- The ':name' and ':email' placeholders are bound to the actual values using the 'execute()' method.
- 'PDO::FETCH_ASSOC' fetches the result as an associative array."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Proper error handling is crucial for robust database interactions. PDO provides exception handling for database errors.  Always wrap your database operations in 'try...catch' blocks."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"<?php
try {
    // Database operations
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    echo "Database error: " . $e->getMessage();
}
?>"},{"order":22,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of connecting to and performing CRUD operations on PostgreSQL and SQLite databases using PHP and PDO. Remember to always use prepared statements to prevent SQL injection and implement proper error handling for robust applications. The key difference lies in the connection string and the database setup (server-based vs. file-based)."}]
